  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given three positive integers X,Y,X, Y, X,Y, and ZZZ.\n\nIn one operation, you can choose any one of these values, and change it to any integer of your choice.\n\nFind the **minimum** number of operations required to make the sequence (X,Y,Z)(X, Y, Z)(X,Y,Z) an arithmetic progression.\n\nNote that (X,Y,Z)(X, Y, Z)(X,Y,Z) is an arithmetic progression if and only if Y−X=Z−YY-X = Z-YY−X=Z−Y.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input contains a single integer TTT, denoting the number of testcases.\nThe first and only line of each testcase contains three space-separated integers X,YX, YX,Y and ZZZ, denoting the three numbers.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print one line containing a single integer: the **minimum** number of operations such that (X,Y,Z)(X, Y, Z)(X,Y,Z) forms an arithmetic progression.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤X,Y,Z≤1001 \\leq X,Y,Z \\leq 1001≤X,Y,Z≤100"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2 5 8\n7 4 3",
        "output": "0\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Y−X=3Y - X = 3Y−X=3 and Z−Y=3Z - Y = 3Z−Y=3 which are already equal. So, (X,Y,Z)(X, Y, Z)(X,Y,Z) is already an arithmetic progression, no moves are required.\nTest case 222: We use one operation and change ZZZ to 111, so that (X,Y,Z)=(7,4,1)(X, Y, Z) = (7, 4, 1)(X,Y,Z)=(7,4,1).\nNow, Y−X=−3Y - X = -3Y−X=−3 and Z−Y=−3Z - Y = -3Z−Y=−3 which are equal, so this is an arithmetic progression."
      }
    ]
  },
